{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: anti-drift lint is one-directional — add the reverse mirror check**

The test proves stage-excludes ⊆ `.pubignore`, but not `.pubignore` ⊆ stage-excludes: a future non-negated entry added to `.pubignore` that the stage script does *not* exclude would make the root dry-run payload *smaller* than the staged publish payload — the dry-run would then no longer "rehearse the payload pub actually ships" (the group's stated contract), and nobody would notice. Today every non-negated entry (`/.git/`, `/.github/`, `/.worktrees/`, `/.trash/`, the 8 sub-project trees, `/vendor/*`, `/packages/*`, `/pubspec_overrides.yaml`) does trace to a stage `--exclude`, so the reverse lint is green now and cheap to add:

```dart
test('every .pubignore exclude mirrors a staging exclude', () {
  final stageBodies = _stagingFilters()
      .where((f) => !f.include)
      .map((f) => f.body)
      .toSet();
  for (final r in _pubIgnoreRules()) {
    if (r.negated) continue;
    expect(
      stageBodies.contains(r.body),
      isTrue,
      reason:
          '.pubignore excludes ${r.line} but the stage script does not — '
          'the root dry-run payload no longer mirrors the staged publish',
    );
  }
});
```

(If a deliberate root-only exclusion ever lands, the test's `reason:` tells the author exactly where to record it.)
